    <section id="manifesto">
      <div class="narrow lede">
        <p class="kicker">01 — Opening</p>
        <p>Halal means permitted under Islamic law. Zabiha is the slaughter strict certifiers demand before they will call the meat halal. Without that rite, the same authorities call ordinary American meat haram. Not unsafe. Not uninspected. Not nutritionally different. Forbidden.</p>
<p>A republic does not take food orders from a forbidden-and-permitted stamp. This page is the commercial half of the refusal. The cut itself is answered at nozabiha.com.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">The grocery warning, in their own words</p>
        <blockquote>Meat that is not slaughtered according to zabiha is typically deemed non-halal. This includes meat from conventional grocery stores, where the slaughter methods may not meet Islamic requirements. Such meat is often considered haram, meaning it is forbidden for Muslims to consume. For Muslims, it is crucial to seek out zabiha-certified meat to ensure compliance with dietary laws.</blockquote>
        <p class="fine">That paragraph is the whole ask: describe a religious killing rule, declare every other carcass out of bounds, and call the declaration a fact. It is a fact about a doctrine. It is not a fact about American meat.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
