          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>What the two words are doing.</h2>
          <p class="deck">Zabiha is the cut. Halal is the verdict. Sellers swap them when it helps the sale, and split them when a stunned bird still needs a sticker. Read the system, not the typeface.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-grid"><article class="claim">
        <p class="num">01</p>
        <h3>The verdict</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">Their claim</p>
        <p>Halal means the food is lawful. Non-zabiha is cast as generally unlawful.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The refusal</p>
        <p>Lawful for a doctrine is not lawful-as-a-fact. Once a package uses “halal” the way other packages use “Grade A,” a religious court has been slipped into the meat case. We will not stock that court as the default.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">02</p>
        <h3>The cut behind the sticker</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">Their claim</p>
        <p>Without zabiha, meat is generally not permissible.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The refusal</p>
        <p>Then say so honestly: the sticker is not a spice blend or a cleanliness claim. It is a claim that the animal was killed under Islamic rules. If those rules were not followed, the hardline product is that the meat is illicit. Ordinary inspected meat is not illicit because a certifier frowned at it.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">03</p>
        <h3>The internal split they hide</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">Their claim</p>
        <p>One Muslim rule, one certified answer, no daylight.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The refusal</p>
        <p>There is daylight. Quran 5:5 calls the food of Jews and Christians lawful. A large share of Muslims in the West eat supermarket meat on that basis. Zabiha agencies exist to close that door and sell the stricter one. A divided theology is being marketed as a single consumer fact.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">04</p>
        <h3>The fee</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">Their claim</p>
        <p>Certification is just assurance, the way any audit is assurance.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The refusal</p>
        <p>Packers pay a religious agency to watch a line and issue a mark. That mark is not a federal grade. The cost does not stay inside the mosque. It lands in separate SKUs, separate kitchens, and separate bids that every shopper and every taxpayer stands downstream of. Nobody voted for a second food code.</p>
      </article></div>
