    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">Procurement is the back door</p>
        <h2>Inclusion that runs one direction is establishment by menu.</h2>
        <p>Watch the public kitchen. A district, a hospital, a jail, or a city agency drops pork, then drops non-halal vendors, then calls the remainder inclusive. The meal has been made lawful for one doctrine, and that doctrine has become the house recipe.</p>
<p>That is not hospitality. Hospitality is an option. A single halal menu is a rule. The child who is not Muslim, the patient who is not Muslim, and the inmate who is not Muslim did not enroll in a dietary law. They were assigned one.</p>
<p>The Humane Methods of Slaughter Act already shows how the rite enters American law: not as the standard, but as an exemption from stunning for religious slaughter. Halal certification is the market version of the same move. First the carve-out. Then the logo. Then the contract that will only buy the logo.</p>
<p>The physical cut is answered on nozabiha.com. Short version: stunning is the Western welfare baseline; a conscious throat-cut is the thing the exemption permits. A gold-green stamp does not change the physiology.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">04</p>
        <h2>Conventional grocery meat is not contraband.</h2>
        <p>Read the pitch again. Meat from conventional grocery stores is “typically deemed non-halal” and “often considered haram,” and it is “crucial” to seek zabiha-certified product.</p>
<p>It is not crucial to anyone except the doctrine making the ruling. Temperature abuse is crucial. Pathogens are crucial. Drug residues are crucial. A slaughterman failing to pronounce a religious formula is not a hazard to the public. It is a missed liturgy.</p>
<p>Treating Kroger, Costco, and the corner market as presumptively unlawful meat is a theological boycott dressed up as consumer education. We reject the education.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <h2>Who the sticker is actually for.</h2>
        <p>If halal were only a voluntary mark for people who want it, this page would be shorter. Buy it, don’t buy it. The growth market is elsewhere: airlines, campuses, hospitals, stadiums, and school bids, where one certified line is cheaper than two and the uncertified line quietly disappears.</p>
<p>Non-Muslim customers then pay for a rite they did not ask for, and they are told that objecting is ignorance. It is not ignorance. It is a refusal to eat under someone else’s religious jurisdiction.</p>
<p>islamophobe.me makes the broader case: a legal-religious system that does not mean to sit politely beside civil law. Industrialterrorism.com is the other sister record. This site stays on the food. The food is enough.</p>
        <p><a href="https://nozabiha.com/">Continue to nozabiha.com</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sand">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">The line in the sand</p>
        <h2>A halal requirement in public institutions is a Muslim practice wearing a procurement form. It is not compatible with Western American values, and it never will be.</h2>
        <div class="prose"><p>One civil food law. Equal service. Animal welfare set by veterinarians and by statute, not by fiqh. No religious test on a lunch tray a child cannot opt out of.</p>
<p>Private houses, private grocers, and private certification for private buyers can continue. The moment the stamp becomes the condition of a public meal, the practice has left the realm of opinion and entered the realm of rule.</p>
<p>We are not compatible with that rule. We are not going to become compatible with it. A person is not the target. The jurisdiction is.</p></div>
